Name: winwrap
Short Name: winwrap
URL: https://github.com/Alex313031/winwrap
Version: N/A
Revision: See the winwrap entry in src/DEPS
License: BSD-3-Clause
License File: LICENSE
Security Critical: yes
Shipped: yes

Description:
Implementations of Win32 functions that older Windows releases do not export,
so a binary compiled against a current SDK still loads on them. A statically
imported symbol the target OS does not export is fatal at load, before any
process code runs, so this cannot be solved at the call site.

Calls are captured without touching call sites: a __declspec(dllimport) call
loads a pointer from __imp_<name>, so defining that symbol here is enough. The
linker resolves it from winwrap and never extracts kernel32.lib's member for
it. What makes winwrap win over kernel32.lib and shell32.lib, both of which GN
names explicitly in `libs`, is that winwrap arrives as a link *input*, and the
response file lists inputs ahead of libs. That is true of the `link` tool as
upstream writes it, but not of `solink`, which lists libs first -- so every DLL
would keep its kernel32 import while executables bound here. Electron Legacy's
winwrap patch gives DLLs the same order; a tree integrating winwrap some other
way has to arrange the same thing.

YY-Thunks (https://github.com/Chuyu-Team/YY-Thunks, MIT) covers much of the
same ground and was consulted while writing several of these functions. It is
cited in the source comments where it mattered. None of its code is used here,
so this is an acknowledgement rather than a license obligation and the license
above is unaffected.

Local Modifications:
None. Written as part of Electron Legacy
(https://github.com/Alex313031/electron-legacy) and split out so it can be
worked on without regenerating a patch for every change, and used by projects
that are not Chromium-based. The GN build here is one integration, not the only
intended one.
